To process a large text response as it arrives instead of waiting for the whole file, you can use the `Response.body` as a `ReadableStream` object and pipe it through a `TextDecoderStream` to get the text content.

Here's an example:
```javascript
const url = "https://www.example.org/a-large-file.txt";

async function fetchTextAsStream(url) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Response status: ${response.status}`);
    }

    const stream = response.body.pipeThrough(new TextDecoderStream());
    for await (const value of stream) {
      console.log(value);
    }
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(e);
  }
}
```
This code creates a `ReadableStream` from the response body and pipes it through a `TextDecoderStream`, which decodes the UTF-8-encoded body data as text. The `for await` loop then iterates over the stream, processing each chunk of the response as it arrives.

Alternatively, you can use the `makeTextFileLineIterator` function to process the response line by line:
```javascript
async function* makeTextFileLineIterator(fileURL) {
  const response = await fetch(fileURL);
  const reader = response.body.pipeThrough(new TextDecoderStream()).getReader();

  let { value: chunk = "", done: readerDone } = await reader.read();

  const newline = /\r?\n/g;
  let startIndex = 0;

  while (true) {
    const result = newline.exec(chunk);
    if (!result) {
      if (readerDone) break;
      const remainder = chunk.slice(startIndex);
      ({ value: chunk, done: readerDone } = await reader.read());
      chunk = remainder + (chunk || "");
      startIndex = newline.lastIndex = 0;
      continue;
    }
    yield chunk.substring(startIndex, result.index);
    startIndex = newline.lastIndex;
  }

  if (startIndex < chunk.length) {
    // Last line didn't end in a newline char
    yield chunk.substring(startIndex);
  }
}

async function run(urlOfFile) {
  for await (const line of makeTextFileLineIterator(urlOfFile)) {
    processLine(line);
  }
}

function processLine(line) {
  console.log(line);
}

run("https://www.example.org/a-large-file.txt");
```
This code creates an iterator that yields each line of the response as it arrives, allowing you to process the response line by line.

To merge two dictionaries, you can use dictionary unpacking (via **) as shown in excerpt [4] [5]:

```
>>> odds = {i: i**2 for i in (1, 3, 5)}
>>> evens = {i: i**2 for i in (2, 4, 6)}
>>> {**odds, **evens}
{1: 1, 3: 9, 5: 25, 2: 4, 4: 16, 6: 36}
```

Alternatively, you can use the dict() constructor with a sequence of key-value pairs, as shown in excerpt [4] [4]:

```
>>> dict([('sape', 4139), ('guido', 4127), ('jack', 4098)])
{'sape': 4139, 'guido': 4127, 'jack': 4098}
```

Note that the page excerpt [4] also mentions that when the keys are simple strings, it is sometimes easier to specify pairs using keyword arguments:

```
>>> dict(sape=4139, guido=4127, jack=4098)
{'sape': 4139, 'guido': 4127, 'jack': 4098}
```

However, this method is not explicitly mentioned as a way to merge two dictionaries.

To log a page visit when the `url` changes but not when only the `shoppingCart` changes, you can use the `useEffect` hook with the `useEffectEvent` hook.

First, declare an `Effect Event` with `useEffectEvent` and move the code reading `shoppingCart` inside of it [5]. This ensures that `shoppingCart` won't re-run your `Effect`.

```javascript
function Page({ url, shoppingCart }) {
  const onVisit = useEffectEvent(visitedUrl => {
    logVisit(visitedUrl, shoppingCart.length)
  });
  useEffect(() => {
    onVisit(url);
  }, [url]); // All dependencies declared
}
```

By doing this, you ensure that the `Effect` only runs when the `url` changes, and not when only the `shoppingCart` changes. This is because `Effect Events` are not reactive and must always be omitted from dependencies of your `Effect`, as stated in [5].

According to the page excerpts, an options object created during rendering makes your Effect reconnect after every commit because the options object is different for every render [2]. This is because the options object is created from scratch on every re-render, and as a result, its dependencies are always different on a commit [2].

To fix this issue, you should create the options object inside the Effect, rather than creating it during rendering [2]. This way, the options object will not change unless you update its dependencies.

For example, instead of this:
```javascript
const options = { // 🚩 This object is created from scratch on every re-render
  serverUrl: serverUrl,
  roomId: roomId
};
```
You should use this:
```javascript
const options = {
  serverUrl: serverUrl,
  roomId: roomId
};
```
Inside the Effect, like this:
```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  const options = {
    serverUrl: serverUrl,
    roomId: roomId
  };
  const connection = createConnection(options);
  connection.connect();
  return () => connection.disconnect();
}, [roomId]);
```
By creating the options object inside the Effect, you ensure that it will not change unless you update its dependencies, which is the `roomId` string in this case. This way, your Effect will only reconnect when the `roomId` changes, and not after every commit.
